+### Adding a second Google account
+
+**Run the same command again with the other address.** It **adds** that account — nothing about the
+account you already connected changes:
+
+```
+richos-service workspace connect google \
+  --account your.other.address@gmail.com \
+  --source calendar --source drive --source mail
+```
+
+No `--client-file` and no `--client-id` the second time: it is the same OAuth app, and its secret is
+already in your Keychain. Google shows you its consent screen again, for the other account, and
+RichOS tells you up front whose grant it is not touching:
+
+```
+account:    your.other.address@gmail.com
+keeping:    you@yourcompany.com  (untouched by this consent)
+```
+
+**The second account must also be a test user of the same project** (Step 3.2). It is the one thing
+that has to happen in the console rather than in the command, and skipping it means Google refuses
+your approval at its own screen with no explanation RichOS can improve on.
+
+From then on `status` and `sync` cover **every** connected account, each reporting its own expiry, its
+own sources and its own counts — `--account <address>` narrows either one to a single account when you
+want that. This matters because your accounts are genuinely different: a Google account built on an
+outside address has your Drive but **no Gmail mailbox at all**, and RichOS says so per account rather
+than calling it an error — `gmail: unavailable — this Google account has no Gmail mailbox` beside the
+other account's real count, in the same pull. Each account keeps its own authorization, its own
+7-day clock, its own place in each source, and its own evidence, so the same document or message
+belonging to two accounts is two items and never one overwriting the other.
+
+`disconnect` is the one command that asks you to be specific: with more than one account connected,
+`richos-service workspace disconnect google` refuses and lists them rather than guessing, and
+`--account <address>` revokes exactly that one.
+
+> **If you authorized RichOS before 17 September 2026**, your `_oauth_client.json` names a single
+> account at its top level. The next command you run rewrites it as a list, in place, saying
+> `config: … now lists accounts (your existing account is unchanged)` — and your existing
+> authorization is carried over, not thrown away. There is nothing for you to do first.
+
